# kprobes samples

The `samples/kprobes/` directory contains simple loadable kernel modules that
exercise kprobe instrumentation in a friendly way.

- `kprobe_example.ko` shows how to install a single probe on `kernel_clone`
  and print register snapshots, as documented in
  `samples/kprobes/kprobe_example.c`.
- `kretprobe_example.ko` demonstrates `kretprobe` usage.
- `kprobe_primary.ko` plants the baseline probe whose log output mirrors
  `kprobe_example`. Keep this module loaded while you add/remove conflict
  probes so the optimizer has something to re-optimize.
- `kprobe_conflict.ko` registers a second probe that targets the same symbol
  (default is `kernel_clone`). Loading it alongside `kprobe_primary.ko`
  forces an optimization conflict; unloading it allows the primary probe to
  reclaim the `OPTIMIZED` state once the optimizer worker runs again.

## Building and running the conflict demo

Enable `CONFIG_SAMPLE_KPROBES` in your kernel configuration and build the
samples:

```bash
make samples/kprobes/kprobe_example.ko samples/kprobes/kprobe_primary.ko \
     samples/kprobes/kprobe_conflict.ko
```

Load the modules in two steps:

```bash
sudo insmod samples/kprobes/kprobe_primary.ko primary_symbol=kernel_clone
sudo insmod samples/kprobes/kprobe_conflict.ko conflict_symbol=kernel_clone
```

Check `dmesg` (or `/sys/kernel/debug/kprobes/list`) to see both handlers firing
and verify that the primary probe gets flagged as `OPTIMIZED`. While the
conflict module is loaded, `kprobe.conflict` temporally occupies the same
address so the optimizer has to unoptimize the first probe. Removing the
conflict probe simulates the TODO in `kernel/kprobes.c::__disarm_kprobe`:

```bash
sudo rmmod kprobe_conflict
cat /sys/kernel/debug/kprobes/list | grep kernel_clone
```

The primary probe should reappear as `OPTIMIZED` after the optimizer worker
finishes processing the deferred work. Finally unload the primary probe when
you’re done:

```bash
sudo rmmod kprobe_primary
```
